The genus Tremella (Basidiomycota, Tremellales) in Finland   Emilia Pippola1,* & Heikki Kotiranta2


1,2$ Basidiomata not macroscopically visible, in the hymenium of other fungi
1,4$ Distinct basidiomata or galls
2,0 $ In the hymenium of polypores $ T. polyporina
2,3$ In the hymenium of heterobasidioid Dacrymyces species
3,0 $ Basidia typically without stalks, conidia present, hyphae with real clamps $ T. obscura
3,0 $ Basidia typically with stalks up to 18 ?m long, conidia absent, hyphae with pseudoclamps $ T. giraffa
4,5$ On lichens
4,10$ On wood or on non-lichenized fungi
5,0 $ Spores typically ellipsoid (Q* = 1.2), basidiomata pale brown to almost black, on Cetrariella delisei or Tuckermanopsis chlorophylla $ T. cetrariicola
5,6$ Spores globose to subglobose, often wider than long (Q* = 0.9), basidiomata or galls on other lichens
6,0 $ Basidia always four-celled, uppermost septum in each basidium longitudinal or oblique and two lower septa transverse, basidiomata pale rose to pale brown, on Ramalina fraxinea $ T. ramalinae
6,7$ Basidia two-celled or very rarely four-celled, all septa parallel, basidiomata or galls on other lichens
7,8$ Basidia typically with one transverse septum, asteroconidia abundant in some specimens
7,9$ Basidia longitudinally or obliquely septate
8,0 $ Basidiomata or galls olive brown to almost black, on Phaeophyscia spp$ T. phaeophysciae
8,0 $ Basidiomata or galls pale brown to reddish brown, on Cladonia spp$ T. cladoniae
9,0 $ Basidial cells elongated, variable in size, basidiomata almost black, on Hypocenomyce scalaris $ T. hypocenomycis
9,0 $ Basidia globose, irregularly ellipsoid, oblong or ovoid, basidiomata or galls pink to brownish orange, on Hypogymnia physodes $ T. hypogymniae
10,11$ Basidiomata mostly more than 1 cm in diam.
10,13$ Basidiomata smaller, only over 1 cm in diam. via coalescence
11,0 $ Basidiomata with a firm core, rose-coloured, cerebriform $ T. encephala
11,12$ Basidiomata lacking a firm core, cream, yellow, brown or almost black, becoming foliose or lobate in maturity
12,0 $ Basidiomata cream, yellow or orange-yellow $ T. mesenterica
12,0 $ Basidiomata pale brown to almost black $ T. foliacea
13,0 $ On Juniperus communis $ T. karstenii
13,14$ On hosts other than Juniperus communis
14,0 $ On Aleurodiscus amorphous on Abies sibirica $ T. mycetophiloides
14,15$ On deciduous trees, usually on or together with pyrenomycetes (Ascomycota)
15,0 $ Basidiomata less than 3 mm in diam., basidia longstalked $ T. globispora
15,0 $ Basidiomata 3-10 mm in diam., basidia mostly stalkless $ T. indecorata
